Comment (internal/api/handler/oscal/system_security_plans.go:null):
The comment above replaceResponsibleRoles says the new roles are appended through the association so GORM fills in parent_id/parent_type, but the implementation now sets parent_id/parent_type explicitly and uses tx.Create(&roles) (not Association(...).Append). Updating the comment will help prevent future refactors from reintroducing the Append behavior that drops responsible_role_parties join rows.
Replies:
**lisa implementation guidance:** Correct — that doc comment is stale and actively dangerous, since it describes the exact `Association(...).Append` approach that was found to silently drop every `responsible_role_parties` join row. Leaving it would invite a future refactor to reintroduce the bug.

FIX in internal/api/handler/oscal/system_security_plans.go, the doc comment directly above `replaceResponsibleRoles`. It currently says the new roles "are appended through the association so GORM fills in parent_id/parent_type — a plain Replace would orphan the old rows rather than delete them". The implementation now sets parent_id/parent_type explicitly (parent_type derived from the parsed GORM schema's table name) and uses `tx.Create(&roles)`.

Rewrite the doc comment to state what the code actually does and, critically, WHY — that `Association("ResponsibleRoles").Append(roles)` writes the role rows but does NOT cascade into each role's own `Parties` many2many, leaving every `responsible_role_parties` join row unwritten, whereas a plain `Create` does cascade (the same path the nested by-component create relies on). Keep the note about old rows going through `deleteResponsibleRoles` so their join rows are cleared first while the shared Party records survive.

The in-body comment on the `tx.Create(&roles)` line already explains this — make the doc comment agree with it rather than duplicating it at length. Comment-only change; no behaviour change, and the existing responsible-role tests already cover the behaviour.

